# ---- GOLDEN FIXTURE (bridge tile) ----
# resetMode=loadvm -> restore the INTERNAL 'golden' snapshot (X started with
# -nocursor + MAME 0.251 `spectrum` already at the machine's power-on screen).
# Nothing is typed after the restore: the mpf2 add proved that post-restore key
# injection races the restore and can leave the exhibit scrolled.
#
# The golden is the screen THE MACHINE CHOSE, not a curated state inside a
# program — the Plus/4 add shipped a golden resting in its spreadsheet and had
# to be re-baked because a visitor arrived mid-application with no idea what it
# was. Here that principle costs nothing and pays twice: the untouched screen is
# also the copyright notice Amstrad's 1999 permission requires to remain
# unaltered, so the exhibit's idle state IS its licence compliance.
#
# A GOLDEN CAN BE PIXEL-PERFECT AND STILL BE BROKEN. A kiosk MAME started at
# cold boot is sometimes born DEAF: X delivers KeyPress/KeyRelease to its window
# (proved with xev), the guest's i8042 interrupt count rises, and MAME passes
# none of it to the emulated machine — not even its own UI menu. Restarting the
# kiosk cures it; savevm/loadvm neither causes nor cures it. The first bake of
# this tile captured such an instance and produced a fixture that restored a
# machine nobody could type on. scripts/build-guests/tiles/zxspectrum.sh now gates the
# bake on a BEHAVIOURAL predicate: type, require the frame to move, soft-reset
# (MAME F3 inside a scroll_lock UI-toggle sandwich) back to a byte-identical
# power-on screen, and only then savevm.
SH_RESET_MODE=loadvm
SH_GOLDEN_SNAPSHOT=golden
SH_FIXTURE_DESC=Sinclair ZX Spectrum 48K at its own untouched power-on screen: the whole 352x296 raster — border included — in the machine's non-bright white, with one line of black text, "© 1982 Sinclair Research Ltd", along the bottom. Drawn by MAME 0.251's `spectrum` driver fullscreen with -keepaspect on the bridge base's stock 1024x768 X root, which the Spectrum's 4:3 picture fills exactly. Nothing is curated and nothing is typed; the copyright line the ROM prints is also the condition under which Amstrad permits the ROM to be emulated at all. Keyboard-only exhibit; no pointer device; 1-bit beeper -> ALSA -> AC97.

# ---- KEYBOARD PACING (the exhibit's only input surface) ----
# The emulated ULA scans the 8x5 keyboard matrix once per 50.08 Hz frame
# (19.97 ms), so the playbook's frame-derived floor would be 40/40. That floor is
# WRONG HERE, for two compounding reasons measured on this tile on 2026-08-09:
#
#  1. MAME does not run at 100% of real time in this guest. `mame spectrum
#     -video none -sound none -str 10` reported 91.7% with nothing else running
#     and 68.9% with the kiosk also up; unthrottled it reaches only 148%. Wall
#     milliseconds therefore buy FEWER emulated frames than the arithmetic says,
#     and the shortfall moves with host load (the box was at load 77 of 16 cores
#     during these measurements — a build storm, but a real operating state).
#
#  2. A REPEATED CHARACTER is the worst case on this machine, and the Spectrum's
#     type-in listings are full of them. The ROM's LAST-K debounce only accepts
#     the same key twice if it sees a genuine "no key pressed" scan in between,
#     so a doubled letter needs the RELEASE, not just the press, to survive a
#     frame. Typing "1122334455" through QMP with explicit press/release pairs:
#
#       hold/gap    characters that landed (of 10)
#        80/ 80     2 of 5 doubles collapsed
#       120/120     7
#       160/160     8
#       200/200     9-10
#       250/250     10 of 10, repeatedly
#
#     A non-repeating 20-character line lost 1 character at 80/80 and none at
#     200/200. 200 ms is ten PAL frames of nominal margin, which is what it
#     takes for the release to be observed when the emulator is running at
#     two-thirds speed under contention.
#
# Re-measured on a soltest CLONE of this tile's own golden with
# scripts/dev/emu-key-pacing-bisect.py (PACE_GRID/PACE_SETTLE_S were added to
# that tool for this run), typing the 40-character line
# "1122334455667788 abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvw" — eight doubled digits and
# twenty-three distinct letters — three times per pacing, box at load ~40-55:
#
#   hold/gap   corrupted lines   what was actually lost
#    80/ 80    3 of 3            7 of the 8 doubles collapsed; ALL 23 letters fine
#   120/120    3 of 3
#   200/200    1 of 3            a single doubled digit
#   250/250    2 of 3            a single doubled digit
#
# The curve FLATTENS after ~200 ms and does not reach zero: the residual failure
# is a host-scheduling stall, not frame quantisation, so paying another 100 ms
# per key buys nothing measurable while making every type-in half as fast again.
# 200/200 is therefore the shipped value, and the registry demoProgram is
# written with NO repeated character in any line so the exhibit's own listing
# never depends on the one case that can still lose a keystroke.
SH_KEY_MIN_HOLD_MS=200
SH_KEY_MIN_GAP_MS=200
# Keep registry demoProgram.perCharMs >= 400 in step with these two: the SPA
# typist waits line.length * perCharMs before submitting the next line, and
# under-waiting builds a backlog whose symptom is the same missing characters.
# scripts/tiles-registry.py validate_demo_pacing enforces it.
#
# No SH_KEY_MAP. The machine does not need a character TRANSLATION — it needs
# characters that do not exist on its keyboard at all. The Spectrum's 40-key
# matrix has no punctuation: every symbol is SYMBOL SHIFT (host RIGHT shift)
# plus a letter, and the SPA's typeText() only ever sends US scancodes with LEFT
# shift. So `"` `;` `=` `,` and the cursor arrows are unreachable from a type-in
# and are carried instead by the SPA's zxspectrum on-screen keyboard profile
# (CAPS SHIFT / SYMBOL SHIFT latches, EXTENDED MODE, and the symbol chords).
# `keyboard.letterCase: upper-only` still applies: an unshifted letter is what
# the exhibit wants, because CAPS SHIFT + a letter is a capital, and in K mode a
# bare letter is a whole BASIC keyword — which is the exhibit.
